hallenge</span>\n      dataset from start to finish. Beginning with raw data ingestion, we loaded both\n      <span style=\"color: #5DCAA5; font-family: monospace;\">train.csv</span> and\n      <span style=\"color: #5DCAA5; font-family: monospace;\">test.csv</span>\n      into pandas DataFrames and performed an initial structural inspection covering shape, column names,\n      data types, and missing value counts. The dataset was found to be\n      <span style=\"color: #5DCAA5; font-weight: 600;\">fully complete with zero null values</span>\n      across all columns, which is ideal for downstream training without any imputation step required.\n      We then moved into feature engineering, where we derived several new columns from the existing data —\n      including <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">puzzle_type</span>,\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">answer_type</span>,\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">prompt_len</span>,\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">answer_len</span>,\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">word_count</span>, and\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">example_count</span>\n      — each designed to expose a different structural dimension of the dataset that would otherwise\n      remain invisible inside the raw text.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Paragraph 2 -->\n  <div style=\"\n    background: #0f0f0f;\n    border: 0.5px solid #1e1e1e;\n    border-left: 2px solid #854F0B;\n    border-radius: 8px;\n    padding: 18px 22px;\n    margin-bottom: 16px;\n  \">\n    <div style=\"\n      color: #555;\n      font-size: 10px;\n      letter-spacing: 0.12em;\n      text-transform: uppercase;\n      margin-bottom: 10px;\n    \">▸ Visualizations Produced</div>\n    <p style=\"\n      color: #888;\n      font-size: 13px;\n      line-height: 1.9;\n      margin: 0;\n    \">\n      With those features in place, we produced a series of\n      <span style=\"color: #ccc; font-weight: 600;\">ten distinct visualizations</span>,\n      each targeting a different dimension of the data. The\n      <span style=\"color: #9F97EC;\">metric cards panel</span>\n      gave a high-level numerical summary of the dataset. The\n      <span style=\"color: #9F97EC;\">donut chart and horizontal bar chart</span>\n      revealed that Bit Manipulation is the dominant puzzle type at roughly\n      <span style=\"color: #ccc;\">43 percent</span>\n      of the training set, followed by Encryption/Cipher at 28 percent and Algebra at 17 percent. The\n      <span style=\"color: #5DCAA5;\">answer type breakdown</span>\n      showed that binary string answers are the most common format, which has direct implications for how\n      the LoRA adapter should handle output formatting. The\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">prompt length histogram</span>\n      confirmed a tight distribution centered around\n      <span style=\"color: #ccc;\">724 characters with a standard deviation of 211</span>,\n      suggesting the prompts were generated from consistent templates. The\n      <span style=\"color: #D85A30;\">answer length histogram</span>\n      showed a strong left skew with most answers being very short — a mean of just\n      <span style=\"color: #ccc;\">12 characters</span>\n      — confirming that the challenge is about rule inference and precise extraction rather than\n      open-ended generation. The\n      <span style=\"color: #9F97EC;\">scatter plot</span>\n      revealed no meaningful correlation between prompt and answer length, while the\n      <span style=\"color: #5DCAA5;\">box plots</span>\n      showed that Cipher puzzles tend to have slightly longer prompts than Bit Manipulation ones.\n      The <span style=\"color: #EF9F27;\">radar chart</span>\n      scored Bit Manipulation highest on rule depth and Cipher highest on ambiguity. The\n      <span style=\"color: #D85A30;\">keyword frequency chart</span>\n      confirmed the templated nature of the prompts, and the final\n      <span style=\"color: #9F97EC;\">correlation heatmap</span>\n      showed strong correlation between prompt length and word count while example count\n      remained largely independent.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Paragraph 3 -->\n  <div style=\"\n    background: #0f0f0f;\n    border: 0.5px solid #1e1e1e;\n    border-left: 2px solid #993C1D;\n    border-radius: 8px;\n    padding: 18px 22px;\n    margin-bottom: 16px;\n  \">\n    <div style=\"\n      color: #555;\n      font-size: 10px;\n      letter-spacing: 0.12em;\n      text-transform: uppercase;\n      margin-bottom: 10px;\n    \">▸ What These Findings Mean for Modeling</div>\n    <p style=\"\n      color: #888;\n      font-size: 13px;\n      line-height: 1.9;\n      margin: 0;\n    \">\n      Taken together, these findings paint a clear picture of what this dataset demands from a fine-tuned model.\n      The model must learn to extract\n      <span style=\"color: #ccc;\">latent transformation rules</span>\n      from a handful of demonstrations and apply them accurately under strict output formatting constraints.\n      Given the domain imbalance toward Bit Manipulation, a\n      <span style=\"color: #EF9F27; font-weight: 600;\">stratified sampling strategy</span>\n      during fine-tuning is strongly recommended to avoid the adapter overfitting to that single category.\n      The short and precise nature of the answers further suggests that training should prioritize\n      <span style=\"color: #5DCAA5;\">exact-match accuracy</span>\n      over fluency metrics, and that the LoRA rank and target modules should be tuned with\n      <span style=\"color: #ccc;\">reasoning depth</span>\n      in mind rather than generation length. The high rule depth score on Bit Manipulation puzzles\n      suggests these benefit most from few-shot demonstrations kept inside the adapter context window.\n      This notebook serves as the complete\n      <span style=\"color: #9F97EC; font-weight: 600;\">analytical foundation</span>\n      for all subsequent modeling decisions in this competition, and every chart and derived feature\n      produced here should be treated as a direct input into hyperparameter selection, data sampling\n      strategy, and evaluation design for the final LoRA submission.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Recommendations -->\n  <div style=\"\n    background: #0f0f0f;\n    border: 0.5px solid #1e1e1e;\n    border-radius: 8px;\n    padding: 18px 22px;\n    margin-bottom: 16px;\n  \">\n    <div style=\"\n      color: #555;\n      font-size: 10px;\n      letter-spacing: 0.12em;\n      text-transform: uppercase;\n      margin-bottom: 14px;\n    \">▸ Recommended Next Steps</div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;\">\n      <div style=\"display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start;\">\n        <span style=\"\n          background:#1a1a2e;border:0.5px solid #534AB7;\n          color:#9F97EC;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;\n          font-size:10px;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px;\n        \">01</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#777;font-size:12px;line-height:1.7;\">\n          Train a LoRA adapter on the full\n          <span style=\"color:#5DCAA5;font-family:monospace;\">train.csv</span>\n          using a Nemotron base model with chain-of-thought prompting enabled to allow the model\n          to reason step by step before producing its final answer.\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start;\">\n        <span style=\"\n          background:#0d1f19;border:0.5px solid #0F6E56;\n          color:#5DCAA5;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;\n          font-size:10px;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px;\n        \">02</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#777;font-size:12px;line-height:1.7;\">\n          Stratify your train/validation split by\n          <span style=\"color:#EF9F27;font-family:monospace;\">puzzle_type</span>\n          to ensure all five domain categories are proportionally represented in both folds\n          and the adapter does not develop a blind spot for underrepresented types.\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start;\">\n        <span style=\"\n          background:#1f1008;border:0.5px solid #854F0B;\n          color:#EF9F27;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;\n          font-size:10px;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px;\n        \">03</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#777;font-size:12px;line-height:1.7;\">\n          Use\n          <span style=\"color:#EF9F27;font-family:monospace;\">example_count</span>\n          as a curriculum learning signal — begin training on puzzles with more embedded examples\n          and progressively introduce puzzles with fewer demonstrations to build generalisation.\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start;\">\n        <span style=\"\n          background:#1f0d0d;border:0.5px solid #993C1D;\n          color:#F09595;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;\n          font-size:10px;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px;\n        \">04</span>\n        <span style=\"color:#777;font-size:12px;line-height:1.7;\">\n          Evaluate exact-match accuracy separately per\n          <span style=\"color:#EF9F27;font-family:monospace;\">answer_type</span>\n          — binary, integer, and text answers should each have their own diagnostic metric\n          to pinpoint where the adapter is failing before final submission.\n        </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Footer -->\n  <div style=\"\n    display: flex;\n    align-items: center;\n    justify-content: space-between;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n    gap: 12px;\n    padding-top: 20px;\n    border-top: 0.5px solid #1e1e1e;\n    margin-top: 4px;\n  \">\n    <div style=\"color:#333;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.06em;\">\n      NVIDIA Nemotron · Reasoning Challenge · Kaggle 2025 · CC BY 4.0\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;\">\n      <span style=\"\n        background:#1a1a2e;border:0.5px solid #534AB7;\n        color:#9F97EC;padding:3px 12px;border-radius:20px;font-size:11px;\n      \">EDA Complete</span>\n      <span style=\"\n        background:#0d1f19;border:0.5px solid #0F6E56;\n        color:#5DCAA5;padding:3px 12px;border-radius:20px;font-size:11px;\n      \">Ready for Training</span>\n      <span style=\"\n        background:#1f1008;border:0.5px solid #854F0B;\n        color:#EF9F27;padding:3px 12px;border-radius:20px;font-size:11px;\n      \">LoRA Adapter Target</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}}]}